\section{Give Each Kind of Edge Its Own Reachability Rule}

A strong field preserves its target whenever the holder is
live. A weak field does not. A surviving holder's weak slot
must be cleared if its referent dies, or updated if the
referent survives and moves. Pierce notes that weak pointers
and finalizers go beyond the simple reachability model of a
store \cite{pierce2002types}. A collector that supports them
has to decide exactly when an operation can still observe an
object, not simply when its bytes become available. In a
moving heap a weak getter must not return a stale address from
before collection. In a concurrent heap, testing that a value
is nonnull and then converting it to a strong reference must
follow a protocol that excludes concurrent reclamation. It must
also account for any marking obligation created by the new strong
root during a concurrent cycle, as discussed in \Cref{ch:ext-416}.

For a cache indexed by object identity, an ordinary strong
value paired with a weak key can accidentally keep the key
alive. A cache entry may describe a method derived from a
type, while the method representation points back to that type.
If the table traces the value unconditionally, the path from
table to method to type defeats the weak key. An ephemeron
gives the pair a conditional rule: if the entry is reachable
and its key has been marked live by other evidence, trace its
value. The entry's conditional edge is not itself evidence
that its key is live. After its value has been marked, that
value's outgoing strong edges are ordinary strong edges.

\Cref{fig:visual-417} shows the cache when no independent
root reaches its key. The solid arrow from the value back to
the key makes no difference unless the value is reached.
If another root reaches the value itself, it can legitimately
keep the key alive and thereby activate the entry.

