% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Derive Ephemeron Liveness to a Fixed Point}

\begin{verbatim}
trace_conditional_edges():
    shade_all_roots()
    repeat:
        drain_strong_worklist()
        # Scans discover weak slots and ephemerons separately.
        progress := false
        for e in discovered_ephemerons:
            if marked(e.key) and not e.activated:
                e.activated := true
                progress := true
                shade(e.value)
    until not progress

process_observers():
    trace_conditional_edges()
    clear_plain_weak_slots_to_unmarked_targets()
    drop_inactive_ephemeron_entries()
    enqueue_and_mark_finalizable_closure()
    retrace_newly_retained_objects_under_finalization_policy()
\end{verbatim}

For example, root \(K_1\) activates an entry whose value
contains a strong pointer to \(K_2\). That, in turn,
activates the entry with key \(K_2\). If the second entry
appears first in a pass, a single scan misses its value.
The loop drains pending strong work and reexamines entries
until no new rule fires. It includes newly discovered
entries, not just those found by the initial root scan.
An entry activated once cannot become inactive during this
nonconcurrent mark. Two entries pointing at one another's
keys with no independent seed stay inactive; following all
their values before checking keys would compute the wrong
fixed point.

Repeated full passes can be quadratic on a long dependency
chain. Index pending entries by key and schedule activation
when a key first becomes marked. An entry found after its
key was marked must still test that condition during
registration. In a moving implementation these decisions can
use a stable mark identity or forwarding metadata for the
duration of the collection. Do not forward a referent merely
to ask whether it is live: a normal forwarding function
creates a copy when none exists, making the query have the
wrong effect.

\section{Specify Finalization Before Exposing It}

Choose observable ordering explicitly. The sketch uses one
possible policy: clear plain weak slots and drop inactive
ephemerons before retaining objects for finalizers. A later
resurrection does not refill a cleared slot or recreate a
removed cache entry. Then take a snapshot of eligible
unmarked finalizable objects, queue those objects as roots,
and trace the closure the callbacks may access. Define how
weak fields and ephemerons newly discovered in that closure
are processed; for example, retain new conditional values
only for keys live after retention, while never rebuilding
previously dropped entries. Other languages choose different
ordering. The test oracle needs the language's policy, not
a universal expectation about which reference clears first.

Callbacks run after collector metadata is consistent. A
callback might allocate, raise an exception, or store its
receiver in a global. Marking the object only for the callback
body but reclaiming its children first makes the body unsafe.
Record finalization state so that a resurrected object is not
automatically queued a second time, and hold queue roots while
callbacks wait or run. Specify what happens if a callback never
returns and whether exceptions are reported. Provide explicit
close or scoped resource management for prompt release; a
tracing collection might never run before process exit.

\section{Test Decisions Rather Than Callback Timing}

Run the ephemeron chain in both entry orders, and check a
cycle of keys with no seed and the same cycle with one root.
After moving survivors, read through both plain weak and
activated conditional slots. In a concurrent API, repeatedly
race a weak upgrade against the last strong release and verify
that success returns an actual strong owner; failure must not
produce a half-installed handle. Use a deterministic hook to
drain the finalizer queue in tests instead of sleeping for
a wall-clock interval. Include a finalizer that allocates and
resurrects, then drop the resurrecting root and verify the
contract for at-most-once invocation. Distinguish an observable
cleared slot from when its old referent's bytes are reused.

An observer registry also raises a reachability question of its
own. If every registered wrapper is rooted by the registry,
wrappers that users have discarded may accumulate without
bound. Decide whether registration holds the wrapper strongly,
tracks only still-live wrappers, or publishes independent
notifications containing no referent. A dead wrapper must
not keep a notification payload alive unless the notification
is itself an intended root. Test dropping wrappers while
their referents remain live, and dropping the referents while
the wrappers remain live. Those cases stress different sides
of the observer contract.

\section{Distinguish Reclamation From Cache Eviction}

An ephemeron can keep a large value alive whenever a small
key remains reachable. This is correct, but it is not an
eviction policy. A runtime that interns classes for the
entire process might retain the values of an ephemeron cache
for the entire process, even if nobody has queried the cache
recently. Use an explicit size or recency policy when memory
depends on bounding the cache. Tests should be able to
distinguish an explicit eviction from a collector removing
an inactive entry, especially if both paths publish a
notification about that entry.

For partial collection, specify what it means for a key in an
uncollected generation to be live. A nursery pass can treat
that key as provisionally live for an entry it encounters.
This preserves a young value that may still be needed by
an old key, but can retain extra data until the next full
cycle. Remember the relevant conditional slots or entries
so the young collector can reach them without treating every
old value as a strong root. Test a cached value that remains
young for several passes and whose old key becomes unreachable
before the next full pass. The full pass, not the minor pass,
must eventually settle the key's actual reachability.
